[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
### Short answer
Yes – you can generate contour lines **on-the-fly** from a `raster-dem` source with the **`maplibre-contour`** library. The library runs a Web-Worker that reads the DEM tiles, extracts elevation isolines, and serves them to MapLibre as a virtual vector source. No pre-built contour tileset is required.

Below is a step-by-step guide, plus tips on DEM encoding, styling, performance, and debugging.

---

## 1. Prerequisites

| What you need | Why |
|---------------|-----|
| **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 5.5** (or the latest stable) | `map.setTerrain`, `color-relief`, `hillshade`, and the `sky` API are only available from v5.5 onward. |
| **A `raster-dem` source** (e.g., Terrarium, Mapbox, or Mapzen encoding) | The contour extractor works on the raw elevation values stored in the DEM tiles. |
| **`maplibre-contour` npm package** (or a bundled copy) | Provides `DemSource` and the protocol that turns DEM tiles into vector contour tiles. |
| **A modern browser** with Web-Worker support (all current browsers have this). | The heavy lifting (marching-squares / iso-line algorithm) runs off-main-thread. |

> **Note:** If you are already using `map.setTerrain()` for 3-D terrain, you can reuse the same DEM source for contours – just add the extra vector source that `maplibre-contour` creates.

---

## 2. Install / import the library

### Using a bundler (Webpack, Vite, etc.)

```bash
npm install maplibre-contour
```

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import mlcontour from 'maplibre-contour';
```

### Using a plain `<script>` tag

```html
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-contour@latest/dist/maplibre-contour.min.js"></script>
<script>
  const { DemSource } = maplibreContour; // the global object is `maplibreContour`
</script>
```

> **Tip:** The minified bundle already contains a tiny Web-Worker; you don’t need to ship a separate worker file.

---

## 3. Create the DEM source (the same one you’ll use for terrain)

```js
// 1️⃣ Add the raster-dem source (you can also add it via a style JSON)
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', // any URL template that returns DEM tiles
  tileSize: 256,
  // IMPORTANT: match the encoding of the source tiles
  //   "terrarium"  – Mapbox Terrarium (RGB → elevation)
  //   "mapbox"    – Mapbox elevation (signed 16-bit)
  //   "mapzen"    – Mapzen (same as Terrarium)
  //   "raw"       – 16-bit signed integer in a single band (e.g., GeoTIFF-like PNG)
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  maxzoom: 12
});
```

If you already have a terrain source in your style JSON, you can skip the `addSource` call and just reference its `id` later.

---

## 4. Spin up a `DemSource` from `maplibre-contour`

```js
// 2️⃣ Create a DemSource that knows how to read the DEM tiles
const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
  // The URL template must be identical to the raster-dem source above.
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  // Must match the encoding you declared for the raster-dem source.
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  // The highest zoom level you expect to request contours for.
  maxzoom: 12,
  // Set to true to run the worker in a separate thread (default true).
  worker: true
});

// Register the worker with the MapLibre instance.
demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);
```

`DemSource` does **not** add a MapLibre source itself; it only prepares the worker and registers a custom protocol (`mlcontour://`) that you will use in the next step.

---

## 5. Add a *virtual* vector source that points to the contour protocol

```js
// 3️⃣ Add a vector source that uses the custom protocol.
map.addSource('contours', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The protocol URL is generated by the DemSource.
  // You can pass options that control contour spacing per zoom.
  tiles: [
    demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
      // ── Contour spacing (in meters) per zoom level ──
      //   key = zoom, value = [minorInterval, majorInterval]
      thresholds: {
        // At low zoom we want sparse contours.
        8:  [200, 1000],
        // At medium zoom we add more detail.
        10: [100, 500],
        // At high zoom we go fine-grained.
        12: [50, 200],
        // You can keep adding higher zooms if your DEM goes that far.
        14: [20, 100]
      },
      // Property names that will appear on each feature.
      elevationKey: 'ele',   // numeric elevation (meters)
      levelKey:     'level' // 0 = minor, 1 = major (useful for styling)
    })
  ],
  // The vector source must know its maxzoom (same as DEM source).
  maxzoom: 12
});
```

**What’s happening under the hood?**

1. When MapLibre asks for a tile (e.g., `z=10, x=523, y=384`), the `mlcontour://` protocol intercepts the request.
2. The worker fetches the corresponding DEM tile(s), runs an iso-line extraction algorithm, and returns a **vector tile** (MVT) containing line features.
3. The vector source then hands those features to the style layer you’ll add next.

---

## 6. Add a line layer to draw the contours

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-lines',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  // The source-layer name is always "contours" for the generated tiles.
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  layout: {
    // Optional: make the lines follow the terrain when 3-D terrain is on.
    'line-cap': 'round',
    'line-join': 'round'
  },
  paint: {
    // Base color – you can also use a data-driven expression on `level`.
    'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.6)',

    // Thicker lines for major contours (level = 1)
    'line-width': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1, 2.0,   // major contour → 2 px
      0, 1.0,   // minor contour → 1 px
      0.5       // fallback
    ],

    // Optional: dash pattern for minor contours
    'line-dasharray': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      0, [2, 2], // minor → dashed
      1, [0, 0], // major → solid
      [0, 0]     // fallback
    ]
  }
});
```

**Styling ideas**

| Property | Example | Effect |
|----------|---------|--------|
| `line-color` | `['interpolate', ['linear'], ['get', 'ele'], 0, '#006837', 2000, '#ffffbf', 4000, '#7f0000']` | Color by elevation (hypsometric). |
| `line-opacity` | `0.7` | Make the lines semi-transparent so underlying imagery shows through. |
| `line-width` | `['interpolate', ['linear'], ['get', 'ele'], 0, 0.5, 4000, 2]` | Thicker lines at higher elevations (optional). |
| `line-blur` | `0.5` | Softens the line edges for a more “hand-drawn” look. |

---

## 7. (Optional) Combine with 3-D terrain & sky

If you already have a 3-D terrain layer, the contours will automatically drape over it:

```js
// Enable terrain (if you haven’t already)
map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });

// Add a sky for a realistic horizon
map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#88ccee',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
  'fog-color': '#cceeff',
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.3,
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4
});
```

Because the contour source is a **vector** source, it respects the terrain mesh and will be rendered in the correct 3-D position.

---

## 8. Full minimal example (HTML)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Runtime Contours</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-contour@latest/dist/maplibre-contour.min.js"></script>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},
      layers: [] // we’ll add everything programmatically
    },
    center: [-122.447, 37.753],
    zoom: 10,
    pitch: 60,
    bearing: -20,
    antialias: true // needed for smooth terrain
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ DEM source (used for terrain & contour extraction)
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
      tileSize: 256,
      encoding: 'terrarium',
      maxzoom: 12
    });

    // 2️⃣ Enable 3-D terrain (optional but nice)
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1.2 });

    // 3️⃣ Add sky/fog (optional)
    map.setSky({
      'sky-color': '#88ccee',
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
      'fog-color': '#cceeff',
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.3,
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4
    });

    // 4️⃣ Set up maplibre-contour
    const demSource = new maplibreContour.DemSource({
      url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
      encoding: 'terrarium',
      maxzoom: 12,
      worker: true
    });
    demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);

    // 5️⃣ Virtual vector source for contours
    map.addSource('contours', {
      type: 'vector',
      tiles: [
        demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
          thresholds: { 8: [200,1000], 10: [100,500], 12: [50,200] },
          elevationKey: 'ele',
          levelKey: 'level'
        })
      ],
      maxzoom: 12
    });

    // 6️⃣ Draw the contour lines
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'contour-lines',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'contours',
      'source-layer': 'contours',
      paint: {
        'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.6)',
        'line-width': [
          'match',
          ['get', 'level'],
          1, 2.0,
          0, 1.0,
          0.5
        ],
        'line-dasharray': [
          'match',
          ['get', 'level'],
          0, [2,2],
          1, [0,0],
          [0,0]
        ]
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser, tilt the map, and you’ll see contour lines draped over the terrain, generated **on demand** as you pan/zoom.

---

## 9. Performance & best-practice notes

| Issue | Why it matters | How to mitigate |
|-------|----------------|-----------------|
| **Worker startup cost** | The first request spawns a Web-Worker and loads the DEM tile(s). | Keep the DEM source at a reasonable `maxzoom` (e.g., 12-13). Pre-fetch a few tiles if you know the initial view. |
| **Too many contour intervals** | Generating many lines per tile can be CPU-heavy, especially on low-end devices. | Use coarser `thresholds` at low zooms; increase density only where you need detail. |
| **Mismatched DEM encoding** | If `encoding` is wrong, elevation values become garbage → no contours or wildly spaced lines. | Verify the source tiles: Terrarium (`png` with RGB → elevation), Mapbox (`png` with signed 16-bit), or raw 16-bit. The same value must be set on both the `raster-dem` source *and* the `DemSource`. |
| **CORS / authentication** | DEM tiles must be fetchable from the worker (no blocked cross-origin requests). | Serve tiles with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` or use a same-origin proxy. |
| **Memory usage** | Each worker holds a cache of decoded DEM tiles. | The library automatically evicts old tiles; you can tune `maxCacheSize` in the `DemSource` constructor if needed. |
| **Interaction with other vector layers** | Contour lines are just another vector layer; they obey the same style cascade. | Place the contour layer **above** any raster imagery but **below** labels or UI overlays. |
| **Terrain vs. contour sync** | If you change the DEM source URL after the contour source is created, the contours will keep using the old tiles. | Re-create the `DemSource` (or call `demSource.updateUrl(newUrl)`) and re-add the vector source when you swap DEMs. |

---

## 10. Debugging checklist

1. **Console errors?**  
   - `unknown property` → you’re using a Mapbox-only property (e.g., `raster-color`).  
   - `Failed to fetch` → CORS or wrong URL.  
   - `Invalid encoding` → DEM tiles are not Terrarium/Mapbox as declared.

2. **No contours appear?**  
   - Verify the DEM source loads correctly (add a temporary `raster` layer pointing at `terrain-dem` to see the raw elevation image).  
   - Check that `demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl)` was called *before* adding the vector source.  
   - Use the browser’s Network tab to see if `mlcontour://` requests are made and what they return (they should be `application/x-protobuf` MVT blobs).

3. **Contours look too dense / too sparse?**  
   - Adjust the `thresholds` object. Remember the values are **meters**, not pixels.  
   - You can also set a global `contourInterval` (minor) and `majorInterval` (major) if you want the same spacing at all zooms.

4. **Performance stalls on pan/zoom**  
   - Reduce `maxzoom` of the DEM source (fewer high-resolution tiles).  
   - Increase `worker: false` only as a last resort (runs on the main thread, which is *slower* but may avoid worker-startup overhead on very low-end devices).  

---

## 11. Alternatives (if you hit a wall)

| Approach | When to consider it | Trade-offs |
|----------|--------------------|------------|
| **Pre-generated vector contour tiles** (e.g., from `tippecanoe` or a GIS pipeline) | You need *very* high-resolution contours at many zoom levels, or you want to serve them from a CDN without a worker. | Requires a build step, extra storage, and you lose the ability to change contour spacing on the fly. |
| **Client-side marching-squares on a raster layer** | Very small DEMs (e.g., a single-image DEM for a local app). | Not scalable; you’d have to write your own algorithm – `maplibre-contour` already does this efficiently. |
| **Server-side contour generation (e.g., MapServer, GeoServer)** | You already have a GIS server and want to keep the heavy lifting off the client. | Adds server load and latency; you lose the “instant-style-change” flexibility of client-side generation. |

---

## 12. TL;DR – Minimal recipe

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import mlcontour from 'maplibre-contour';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({ /* container, style, etc. */ });

map.on('load', () => {
  // DEM source (used for terrain & contour extraction)
  map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
    type: 'raster-dem',
    url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
    encoding: 'terrarium',
    maxzoom: 12
  });

  // Optional 3-D terrain
  map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

  // Set up contour worker
  const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
    url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
    encoding: 'terrarium',
    maxzoom: 12,
    worker: true
  });
  demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);

  // Virtual vector source for contours
  map.addSource('contours', {
    type: 'vector',
    tiles: [
      demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
        thresholds: { 8: [200,1000], 10: [100,500], 12: [50,200] },
        elevationKey: 'ele',
        levelKey: 'level'
      })
    ],
    maxzoom: 12
  });

  // Draw the lines
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'contour-lines',
    type: 'line',
    source: 'contours',
    'source-layer': 'contours',
    paint: {
      'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.6)',
      'line-width': [
        'match', ['get','level'], 1, 2, 0, 1, 0.5
      ],
      'line-dasharray': [
        'match', ['get','level'], 0, [2,2], 1, [0,0], [0,0]
      ]
    }
  });
});
```

That’s it – you now have **runtime-generated contour lines** that stay in sync with your DEM, work together with 3-D terrain, and can be styled just like any other vector layer. Happy mapping!

Below is a **step-by-step recipe** for turning the empty “page-background” you see above the horizon into a real sky with haze (fog) that blends into the terrain.  
All of the pieces are native to **MapLibre GL JS** – there is no `map.setFog()` (that belongs to Mapbox GL JS) and there is no “sky” layer type. The sky is a **root-level style object** that you add with `map.setSky()` (or by putting a `sky` object in the style JSON).

---

## 1. Prerequisites – make sure the terrain is really on

```js
// 1️⃣ Add the DEM source (raster-dem)
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  tileSize: 256,
  // encoding must match the tiles you serve (terrarium, mapbox, etc.)
  // encoding: 'terrarium'   // <-- uncomment if you use Terrarium tiles
});

// 2️⃣ Turn the DEM into 3-D terrain
map.setTerrain({
  source: 'terrain-dem',
  exaggeration: 1.0          // raise only if you really need it
});
```

> **Why this matters** – The sky (and its fog) is only drawn when a *terrain* object exists. If `setTerrain()` has never been called, the map behaves like a flat 2-D map and the sky will never appear.

---

## 2. Pitch the camera (you already do)

```js
map.easeTo({ pitch: 70, bearing: 0, duration: 0 });
```

> The sky is rendered **behind** the map canvas, but it is only visible when the camera is pitched away from `0°`. A pitch of `> 0` (your 70°) is perfect.

---

## 3. Add a sky object – the “real sky”

```js
// 3️⃣ Add a sky (this replaces the old Mapbox setFog call)
map.setSky({
  // ---- colour of the dome at the very top (zenith) ----
  'sky-color': '#199EF3',          // a light blue – change to whatever you like

  // ---- colour where the sky meets the horizon ----
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',      // usually a brighter/whiter tone

  // ---- how quickly the sky colour blends into the horizon ----
  // 0 = abrupt line, 1 = very gradual
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,

  // ---- fog (haze) that appears when you look toward the ground ----
  // The fog colour is what you see “through” the atmosphere.
  'fog-color': '#cceeff',          // a pale blue-white works well

  // How much the fog colour mixes with the horizon colour
  // (0 = no fog at the horizon, 1 = horizon fully fogged)
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,

  // How much the fog colour mixes with the ground colour
  // (0 = no fog near the ground, 1 = ground fully fogged)
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
});
```

### What each property does

| Property | Effect | Typical range |
|----------|--------|---------------|
| `sky-color` | Colour of the sky at the zenith (top of the dome). | Any CSS colour. |
| `horizon-color` | Colour exactly at the horizon line. | Usually a lighter version of `sky-color`. |
| `sky-horizon-blend` | Smoothness of the gradient from sky-color to horizon-color. | `0 … 1` (0 = hard line). |
| `fog-color` | Colour of the atmospheric haze that appears when you look down. | Light blues, greys, or even a warm pink for sunrise. |
| `horizon-fog-blend` | How much fog is mixed into the horizon colour. | `0 … 1`. |
| `fog-ground-blend` | How much fog is mixed into the ground colour (the terrain). | `0 … 1`. |

> **Tip:** Start with the values shown above, then tweak the three “blend” numbers until the transition looks natural for your data and map style.

---

## 4. Make sure the background layer does not hide the sky

If you have a `background` layer in your style that is opaque (e.g. `background-color: #fff`), it will **cover the sky** because the background is drawn *before* the sky. There are two ways to avoid that:

1. **Remove the background layer** (or set its opacity to `0`), **or**
2. **Place the background layer after the sky** by moving it to the very top of the layer stack (using `moveLayer`).

```js
// Example: make the background transparent
map.setPaintProperty('background', 'background-color', 'rgba(0,0,0,0)');
```

> In most cases you can simply delete the background layer from the style JSON – the sky will then be visible automatically.

---

## 5. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – 3D terrain with sky & fog</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // ---- DEM source (replace with your own URL) ----
        'terrain-dem': {
          type: 'raster-dem',
          url: 'https://tiles.example.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
          tileSize: 256,
          // encoding: 'terrarium'   // uncomment if needed
        },
        // ---- Optional raster imagery source (e.g. satellite) ----
        'sat': {
          type: 'raster',
          url: 'https://tiles.example.com/satellite/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg',
          tileSize: 256
        }
      },
      layers: [
        // Base imagery (optional)
        { id: 'sat', type: 'raster', source: 'sat' },

        // ---- Hillshade (optional, just to show it works with sky) ----
        {
          id: 'hillshade',
          type: 'hillshade',
          source: 'terrain-dem',
          paint: {
            'hillshade-method': 'multidirectional',
            'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.4,
            'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map',
            'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(30,40,80,0.45)',
            'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,240,200,0.4)'
          }
        }

        // No background layer – sky will be visible
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 70,
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true   // required for 3-D terrain
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Enable terrain
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

    // 2️⃣ Add the sky + fog
    map.setSky({
      'sky-color': '#199EF3',
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
      'fog-color': '#cceeff',
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page, tilt the map, and you should see:

* A blue dome overhead (`sky-color`).
* A bright horizon line that smoothly blends into the dome.
* A gentle haze that becomes denser as you look toward the ground (the fog).

---

## 6. Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Only a white/blank background above the horizon** | No `sky` object, or you called `map.setFog()` (which doesn’t exist). | Use `map.setSky()` as shown. |
| **Sky still hidden** | An opaque `background` layer sits on top of the sky. | Remove the background layer or set its opacity to `0`. |
| **Fog never appears** | Terrain not enabled, or `fog-color` left at default (`transparent`). | Ensure `map.setTerrain()` is called *before* `setSky()`, and give `fog-color` a visible value. |
| **Sky colour looks washed-out** | `sky-horizon-blend` set to `1` (full blend) or `sky-color` too close to `horizon-color`. | Reduce `sky-horizon-blend` (e.g., `0.3-0.6`) and pick a slightly darker `sky-color`. |
| **Performance drops heavily** | Too many high-resolution DEM tiles + many overlay layers. | Keep the DEM zoom level reasonable (e.g., `maxzoom: 12`), limit hillshade/contour layers, and enable `antialias: true` only when needed. |
| **Fog looks like a solid band at a fixed height** | `horizon-fog-blend` or `fog-ground-blend` set to `0` or `1`. | Use values between `0.3` and `0.7` for a gradual transition. |

---

## 7. Quick cheat-sheet (copy-paste)

```js
// After map has loaded and terrain is on:
map.setSky({
  'sky-color':            '#199EF3',   // zenith
  'horizon-color':        '#ffffff',   // horizon line
  'sky-horizon-blend':    0.5,         // gradient smoothness
  'fog-color':            '#cceeff',   // haze colour
  'horizon-fog-blend':    0.5,         // fog at horizon
  'fog-ground-blend':     0.5          // fog near the ground
});
```

Adjust the colour strings and blend numbers to match the visual style you want.

---

### TL;DR

1. **Enable terrain** (`map.setTerrain({source:'terrain-dem'})`).  
2. **Pitch the map** (`pitch > 0`).  
3. **Call `map.setSky()`** with `sky-color`, `horizon-color`, `sky-horizon-blend`, `fog-color`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend`.  
4. **Remove or make transparent any opaque background layer** that would hide the sky.

That’s all you need to replace the flat page background with a proper sky and atmospheric haze in MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping!

Below is a **step-by-step recipe** for putting a pre-rendered shaded-relief JPEG tile set under all of your vector data (including the label layer that you want on top).  
The only thing you need is a **raster source** that points at the JPEG tiles and a **raster layer** that draws that source.  
Everything else (ordering, opacity, fade-in, etc.) is handled with a few MapLibre-GL-JS calls.

---

## 1.  Add the raster source

```js
// 1️⃣  Create a raster source that points at your JPEG tiles
map.addSource('relief-basemap', {
  type: 'raster',                     // ← tells MapLibre this is image tiles
  tiles: [
    // Use the URL pattern your tile server expects.
    // {z}/{x}/{y} will be replaced by the map’s zoom, column and row.
    'https://my.tileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'
  ],
  tileSize: 256,                      // most JPEG tiles are 256 px; change if 512
  // optional – give credit that will appear in the map UI
  attribution: '© Your-Provider 2024'
});
```

> **Why `type: 'raster'`?**  
> A pre-rendered JPEG is just an image. It is *not* a DEM, so none of the hillshade / color-relief / terrain APIs apply. The raster source tells MapLibre to fetch the image tiles and turn them into a texture that a `raster` layer can draw.

---

## 2.  Add the raster layer

```js
// 2️⃣  Draw the source as a raster layer
map.addLayer({
  id: 'relief-basemap-layer',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'relief-basemap',
  // Paint properties are optional but useful
  paint: {
    // Keep the image fully opaque (set <1.0 if you want it semi-transparent)
    'raster-opacity': 1.0,

    // Turn off the default 300 ms fade-in if you prefer instant tile swaps
    'raster-fade-duration': 0,

    // If you need a different resampling method (e.g. for high-dpi screens)
    // 'raster-resampling': 'linear'   // or 'nearest'
  }
});
```

> **What this does:**  
> The layer draws the JPEG tiles exactly as they come from the server. No shading, no elevation, no color-relief – just the picture you already prepared.

---

## 3.  Put the raster *under* everything else

MapLibre draws layers in the order they appear in the style’s `layers` array.  
You have two common ways to guarantee the basemap stays at the bottom:

### 3-a.  Add the raster **first**, then add all other layers

If you are building the style programmatically, simply call `addLayer` for the raster **before** you add any vector or label layers.

```js
// Example order
addRasterBasemap();          // ← step 1-2
addVectorLanduse();         // fill, line, etc.
addVectorRoads();           // line
addLabelLayer();            // symbol (labels)
```

### 3-b.  Move the raster layer **behind** a known layer

If the style already exists (e.g. you loaded a JSON style that already contains many layers) you can reposition the raster after you add it:

```js
// Suppose the label layer in the style is called 'place-labels'
map.moveLayer('relief-basemap-layer', 'place-labels'); // puts raster *below* that layer
```

`moveLayer` takes the id of the layer you want to move and the id of the layer **above** which it should be placed.  
If you want the raster at the very bottom, you can pass `null` as the second argument:

```js
map.moveLayer('relief-basemap-layer', null); // now it is the first layer in the stack
```

---

## 4.  (Optional) Add a fallback background

If the raster tiles are missing for some zoom levels or the server returns 404, the map will show a transparent background. Adding a simple background layer gives you a solid colour underneath.

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'fallback-bg',
  type: 'background',
  paint: { 'background-color': '#e0e0e0' }   // light gray, or any colour you like
}, 'relief-basemap-layer'); // insert *before* the raster layer
```

---

## 5.  Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Shaded-relief JPEG basemap + vector labels</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},          // we’ll add sources programmatically
      layers: []            // start empty
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣  raster source
    map.addSource('relief-basemap', {
      type: 'raster',
      tiles: ['https://my.tileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'],
      tileSize: 256,
      attribution: '© Your-Provider 2024'
    });

    // 2️⃣  raster layer (added first → bottom of stack)
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'relief-basemap-layer',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'relief-basemap',
      paint: {
        'raster-opacity': 1,
        'raster-fade-duration': 0
      }
    });

    // 3️⃣  Example vector source (replace with your own)
    map.addSource('roads', {
      type: 'vector',
      url: 'mapbox://mapbox.mapbox-streets-v8'   // just an example
    });

    // 4️⃣  Vector line layer (drawn *above* the raster)
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'road-lines',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'roads',
      'source-layer': 'road',
      paint: {
        'line-color': '#ffffff',
        'line-width': 2
      }
    });

    // 5️⃣  Symbol (label) layer – topmost
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'place-labels',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'roads',
      'source-layer': 'place',
      layout: {
        'text-field': ['get', 'name_en'],
        'text-size': 12,
        'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular']
      },
      paint: {
        'text-color': '#000000'
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace the URLs, source-layer names, and styling to match your own vector data.*

---

## 6.  Quick checklist

| ✅ What you need | ✅ How to do it |
|-----------------|----------------|
| **JPEG tiles** | `type: 'raster'` source with `tiles: ['…/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg']` |
| **Draw them** | `type: 'raster'` layer, optional `raster-opacity`, `raster-fade-duration` |
| **Put them under everything** | Add the raster layer **first** *or* `map.moveLayer('relief-basemap-layer', null)` |
| **Labels on top** | Ensure your label (`symbol`) layer is added **after** the raster layer (or move it above with `moveLayer`) |
| **Optional smoothness** | Set `'raster-fade-duration': 0` for instant tile swaps, or keep the default 300 ms for a gentle fade |
| **Fallback colour** | Add a `background` layer before the raster if you want a solid colour when tiles are missing |

---

### TL;DR

1. **Create a raster source** that points at the JPEG tiles.  
2. **Add a raster layer** that uses that source.  
3. **Place the raster layer at the bottom** of the layer stack (add it first or `moveLayer` it).  
4. **Add your vector and label layers after** (or move them above).  

That’s all you need to get a beautiful pre-rendered shaded-relief basemap behind your interactive vector data. Happy mapping! 🚀

## TL;DR  
`map.setFog()` does not exist in MapLibre GL JS.  
Haze (fog) is now part of the **sky** object.  
Add a sky (via `map.setSky()` or directly in the style) and give it a `fog-color` (plus the two blend
properties). Fog only renders when the map is in **3-D** – i.e. you have a `raster-dem` source
and `map.setTerrain()` **or** the camera is pitched > 0.

Below is a complete, copy-and-paste ready example that reproduces the classic Mapbox “fog at the
horizon” effect.

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – horizon fog</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map (any style you like – here we use a simple
  //     OSM raster base layer)
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // a cheap raster basemap – replace with your own source if you wish
        'osm': {
          type: 'raster',
          tiles: ['https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
          tileSize: 256,
          attribution: '© OpenStreetMap contributors'
        },
        // DEM source – required for fog (see step 3)
        'terrain-dem': {
          type: 'raster-dem',
          url: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain-tiles/tiles.json',
          // encoding must match the source (Terrarium, Mapbox, etc.)
          // default is "mapbox"
        }
      },
      layers: [
        { id: 'osm', type: 'raster', source: 'osm' }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 45,               // 👈 pitch > 0 makes the horizon visible
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true          // required for smooth 3-D rendering
  });

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Enable 3-D terrain (required for fog to appear)
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Add the sky (this is where the fog lives)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    map.setSky({
      // sky-color is the colour of the upper atmosphere
      'sky-color': '#88ccee',          // light blue
      // horizon-color is the colour where sky meets the ground
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',      // white / near-horizon
      // fog-color is the colour of the haze that blends into the horizon
      'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',          // a slightly desaturated blue
      // how quickly the sky colour blends into the fog
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,        // 0 = abrupt, 1 = very smooth
      // how quickly the fog colour blends into the horizon line
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
      // how quickly the fog colour blends into the ground (terrain)
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.5,
      // optional – make the sky look more “atmospheric”
      'atmosphere-blend': 0.25
    });

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  (Optional) Add a hillshade or color-relief layer so you can see
    //     the terrain underneath the fog.
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.4,
        'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map',
        'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(30,40,80,0.45)',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,240,200,0.4)',
        'hillshade-method': 'multidirectional'
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What changed compared to `map.setFog()`?

| Mapbox GL JS v2 (`setFog`) | MapLibre GL JS (v4) |
|----------------------------|---------------------|
| `map.setFog({ "color": "#c0d8ff", "highColor": "#fff", "spaceColor": "#88ccee", "horizonBlend": 0.5 })` | `map.setSky({ "fog-color": "#c0d8ff", "sky-color": "#88ccee", "horizon-color": "#fff", "sky-horizon-blend": 0.5, "horizon-fog-blend": 0.5, "fog-ground-blend": 0.5 })` |
| Fog is a *stand-alone* object. | Fog lives inside the **sky** object. |
| Works on a flat map (no terrain required). | Fog only renders when **terrain is present** (a `raster-dem` source + `map.setTerrain()`) **or** the camera is pitched. |

### Step-by-step checklist

1. **Add a `raster-dem` source** (or any DEM you already use for terrain).  
   Without a DEM the sky will render, but the fog will be invisible.

2. **Enable terrain** with `map.setTerrain({ source: 'your-dem', exaggeration: 1 })`.  
   The `exaggeration` can be > 1 if you want more dramatic relief.

3. **Pitch the map** (`pitch` > 0) – the fog is only visible when the horizon is in view.  
   If you keep `pitch: 0` you’ll just see a flat blue background.

4. **Call `map.setSky()`** (or embed a `sky` object in your style JSON).  
   The only required property for fog is `fog-color`; the three blend properties
   (`sky-horizon-blend`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend`) control how smooth the
   transition is.

5. (Optional) **Add a hillshade or color-relief layer** so the terrain texture is visible
   under the fog. This is the same layer you would have used before the migration.

### Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---------|-------|-----|
| **No haze at all** | No terrain source, or `map.setTerrain()` never called. | Add a `raster-dem` source and call `map.setTerrain()`. |
| **Haze appears but looks like a solid colour** | `pitch` is `0` (camera looking straight down). | Set `pitch` to ≥ 30 ° (`map.easeTo({pitch:45})`). |
| **Fog is too faint / too strong** | Blend values are too low/high. | Tweak `sky-horizon-blend`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend` (range 0-1). |
| **Fog colour is wrong** | Using a Mapbox-only property (`color`, `highColor`, `spaceColor`). | Use the MapLibre equivalents (`fog-color`, `sky-color`, `horizon-color`). |
| **Console error “unknown property: fog-color”** | Using a version of MapLibre older than v4 (sky was added in v4). | Upgrade to MapLibre ≥ 4.0 (current stable is 4.4 at time of writing). |
| **Fog disappears after `map.setTerrain(null)`** | Terrain was turned off. | Keep terrain enabled, or accept that fog only works with terrain. |

### Replicating the exact Mapbox default fog

Mapbox GL JS v2 used the following defaults (see the Mapbox docs):

```js
map.setFog({
  color: '#c0d8ff',          // fog colour
  highColor: '#fff',         // sky colour
  spaceColor: '#88ccee',     // horizon / space colour
  horizonBlend: 0.5
});
```

The equivalent MapLibre call is:

```js
map.setSky({
  'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',
  'sky-color': '#88ccee',
  'horizon-color': '#fff',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.5,
  // optional – makes the sky look a bit more atmospheric
  'atmosphere-blend': 0.25
});
```

Feel free to adjust the colours or blend values to match your design.

### Quick “add-fog-to-any-existing-style” snippet

If you already have a MapLibre map with terrain and just need to inject fog, run this once
after the map has loaded:

```js
if (map.getTerrain()) {               // make sure terrain is on
  map.setSky({
    'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',
    'sky-color': '#88ccee',
    'horizon-color': '#fff',
    'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
  });
}
```

That’s it – the horizon haze you were used to in Mapbox GL JS will now appear in MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping!
